    "revise": "You are the FIGURE REVISER (Opus) for \"The LLM Stack\". Chapter: content/14-capstone/04-architecture.md\nThe chapter's core intuitions: At a fixed parameter budget the body scales as ~12.25 d^2 L, so the product d^2*L is pinned: you buy width or depth with the same currency. At small scale (100M) depth wins because capability is bottlenecked by the number of sequential nonlinear transformations the residual stream can undergo, not the width of each \u2014 hence deep-and-thin (512 wide, 30 layers, width-per-layer ~17 vs GPT-2's ~64).; A handful of big tensors dominate a 100M model, so cheap-but-good choices compound: tying embeddings saves ~16.8M (a sixth of the model), a 32k vs 50k vocab frees ~9M (~3 layers), GQA shrinks W_K/W_V 4x \u2014 and every freed parameter is spent on more depth. ~17% of params live in the embedding, ~83% in the body, by design.; GQA is the middle of the MHA<->MQA spectrum: 8 query heads share 2 KV heads (4:1 groups), so only 2 heads' worth of K,V are stored/moved instead of 8 \u2014 a 4x smaller KV cache (~30 KB/token vs ~120 KB) at near-MHA quality. The KV heads are the memory-expensive part; queries are free.; Attention-logit blowup is the canonical high-LR death: as W_Q,W_K grow, the scaled dot product q.k can exceed ~100, softmax collapses to a hard argmax, gradient to the losing logits ~e^-100 ~ 0, learning freezes and the run NaNs. QK-norm makes it structurally impossible: RMS-normalizing q and k fixes ||q||,||k||~sqrt(d_h)=8, so by Cauchy-Schwarz |q.k|<=64 and after 1/sqrt(d_h) scaling the logit is bounded by ~8 by construction \u2014 this is what buys the aggressive Muon learning rate.\n\nReview each of these figures (the ones this chapter just drew/edited):\n  deep-thin-budget-trade, param-budget-allocation, gqa-head-sharing-kv-cache, qk-norm-logit-bound, rope-nope-layer-stack\n\nFor EACH: render and SEE it \u2014 `python3 scripts/preview_fig.py <NAME> --theme both` from /local-ssd/pk669/programming/llm-stack-textbook,\nthen view /tmp/figpreview/<NAME>.light.png and .dark.png. Judge strictly:\n  - Does it actually BUILD INTUITION for its concept (not just decorate)? Is it correct vs the chapter?\n  - Theme-safe (no hardcoded primary color; readable in BOTH light and dark)?\n  - Nothing clipped by the viewBox; no illegible overlaps; no tofu glyphs (ASCII only in the SVG)?\n  - Marker present in the chapter exactly once; figcaption has a bold lead + takeaway?\nIf a figure has ANY problem, FIX it: Edit /local-ssd/pk669/programming/llm-stack-textbook/figures/<NAME>.html (and re-render to confirm), or\nfix a missing/duplicate marker in the chapter. Re-verify after fixing.\n\nSet verdict per figure: good / fixed / still-broken / missing. `passed` = every figure good-or-fixed\nAND present. Return the structured object."
