    "revise": "You are the FIGURE REVISER (Opus) for \"The LLM Stack\". Chapter: content/14-capstone/10-agentic-narrow.md\nThe chapter's core intuitions: At 100M, distillation is the ONLY path to multi-step tool use: the model learns to REPRODUCE a narrow, well-worn groove of demonstrated ReAct trajectories, not to REASON ABOUT tool use. Few-shot prompting fails in canonical ways (hallucinated observations, ignored results, infinite re-search loops); SFT on verified traces succeeds inside that groove.; Trajectory distillation, not logit distillation: we imitate the teacher's TEXT (its thoughts, tool calls, and final answers) as ordinary SFT targets. So any strong model can teach, no shared tokenizer or lockstep querying is needed, and the student trains with its plain cross-entropy loop.; The pipeline is rejection sampling against a verifiable reward: sample MANY diverse teacher rollouts per task (temperature>0), keep only the ones whose final answer exactly matches gold, dedup, reformat, SFT. The scarce resource is DIVERSE WINNING TRACES, not compute. The same exact-match reward is used offline to filter and online in RLVR to estimate advantages.; The wire format must be brutally regular so a tiny model memorizes ONE grammar: a single canonical JSON tool-call schema with fixed key order {tool, args}, tools wrapped in <|tool_call|>/<|tool_result|>, and the final answer delimited by a literal 'Answer:' so a verifier extracts it with a trivial regex.\n\nReview each of these figures (the ones this chapter just drew/edited):\n  react-wire-format-loss-mask, distill-rejection-funnel, distilled-groove-vs-skill, acc-decomposition-query-bottleneck\n\nFor EACH: render and SEE it \u2014 `python3 scripts/preview_fig.py <NAME> --theme both` from /local-ssd/pk669/programming/llm-stack-textbook,\nthen view /tmp/figpreview/<NAME>.light.png and .dark.png. Judge strictly:\n  - Does it actually BUILD INTUITION for its concept (not just decorate)? Is it correct vs the chapter?\n  - Theme-safe (no hardcoded primary color; readable in BOTH light and dark)?\n  - Nothing clipped by the viewBox; no illegible overlaps; no tofu glyphs (ASCII only in the SVG)?\n  - Marker present in the chapter exactly once; figcaption has a bold lead + takeaway?\nIf a figure has ANY problem, FIX it: Edit /local-ssd/pk669/programming/llm-stack-textbook/figures/<NAME>.html (and re-render to confirm), or\nfix a missing/duplicate marker in the chapter. Re-verify after fixing.\n\nSet verdict per figure: good / fixed / still-broken / missing. `passed` = every figure good-or-fixed\nAND present. Return the structured object."
