    "revise": "You are the FIGURE REVISER (Opus) for \"The LLM Stack\". Chapter: content/14-capstone/12-retrospective-and-scaleup.md\nThe chapter's core intuitions: The '~$100 model' is an accounting, not a slogan: it itemizes to ~39 GPU-hours across all stages (~$1.80/GPU-hr) plus ~$13 non-GPU (teacher API + storage) plus a ~25% re-run reality tax; pretraining is ~40% of the bill and OVER-TRAINING is ~90% of the pretraining line by deliberate choice.; Over-training is inference-aware, not wasteful: you pay training compute ONCE (6ND) but save inference compute (2ND per request) FOREVER; because served FLOPs dwarf the one-time pretrain, the extra ~$36 spent over-training buys a permanently cheaper-to-serve model and pays for itself many times over at deployment scale.; Cost = FLOPs / (MFU x peak): the 6ND rule fixes the FLOP floor; MFU is the sustained/peak fraction (deep-thin Stack-100M sits low, ~0.45); always compute the bill from MEASURED tokens/sec and quote $/GPU-hr AND MFU alongside the dollar figure or it is not reproducible.; Reproducibility = five pillars welded into a self-describing checkpoint: seed STATE (not just the integer), config hash, content-addressed data manifest, environment pin (fail on a dirty git tree), and atomic checkpoints - all validated by a hermetic 50-step CPU run that must reproduce bit-for-bit.\n\nReview each of these figures (the ones this chapter just drew/edited):\n  capstone-cost-anatomy, capstone-pay-once-save-forever, capstone-scaleup-ladder, capstone-moe-capacity-vs-compute\n\nFor EACH: render and SEE it \u2014 `python3 scripts/preview_fig.py <NAME> --theme both` from /local-ssd/pk669/programming/llm-stack-textbook,\nthen view /tmp/figpreview/<NAME>.light.png and .dark.png. Judge strictly:\n  - Does it actually BUILD INTUITION for its concept (not just decorate)? Is it correct vs the chapter?\n  - Theme-safe (no hardcoded primary color; readable in BOTH light and dark)?\n  - Nothing clipped by the viewBox; no illegible overlaps; no tofu glyphs (ASCII only in the SVG)?\n  - Marker present in the chapter exactly once; figcaption has a bold lead + takeaway?\nIf a figure has ANY problem, FIX it: Edit /local-ssd/pk669/programming/llm-stack-textbook/figures/<NAME>.html (and re-render to confirm), or\nfix a missing/duplicate marker in the chapter. Re-verify after fixing.\n\nSet verdict per figure: good / fixed / still-broken / missing. `passed` = every figure good-or-fixed\nAND present. Return the structured object."
